DISCLOSURE
==========

Required declaration under npm's Dual-Use Content Policy.
  Policy : https://docs.npmjs.com/policies/dual-use
  Field  : package.json -> "contentPolicy": { "class": "dual-use" }

Package : @jshookmcp/jshook
License : AGPL-3.0-only
Repo    : https://github.com/vmoranv/jshookmcp

This file describes the security-relevant capabilities this package
intentionally provides, and the legitimate purposes for which it is intended.
It exists so that npm's automated scanning and Trust & Safety review can
distinguish this package from malware that happens to use similar techniques.


1. WHAT THIS PACKAGE IS
-----------------------

`@jshookmcp/jshook` is a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server. It exposes a set of
tools that an AI assistant (or a human operator driving one) can call to perform
JavaScript analysis and browser-side security research.

It is a developer / researcher tool that the operator runs locally against
targets they choose. It is not a library that executes anything on its own, it
does not run as a service on behalf of a third party, and it does not contact
any server we control. It ships as a CLI (`jshook` / `jshookmcp`) plus an MCP
stdio server.


2. WHY IT IS DUAL-USE
---------------------

The techniques below are the standard toolkit of JavaScript security research.
They are also the standard toolkit of malicious JavaScript. An automated scanner
cannot tell the two apart from code alone, which is precisely the situation
npm's dual-use policy exists to handle.

Declared capabilities, stated plainly:

  - JavaScript hooking and function instrumentation. Intercepting, wrapping and
    observing function calls in a running page, including replacing
    implementations and reading arguments and return values.
  - Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) control. Attaching to a browser over CDP,
    evaluating expressions in a page context, enumerating targets, workers and
    scripts, and setting breakpoints.
  - Network monitoring and interception. Observing and modifying request and
    response traffic produced by the browser under analysis.
  - TLS key logging and decryption. Chrome can be launched with a TLS key log
    file; a companion tool parses NSS-format key material (CLIENT_RANDOM /
    TRAFFIC_SECRET) so that captured traffic from the operator's own browser
    session can be decrypted. This applies only to traffic the operator's own
    browser produces.
  - Deobfuscation and static analysis. Reversing minified, packed or
    control-flow-flattened JavaScript, including AST-based analysis and
    cryptography detection.
  - Anti-detection / stealth browser configuration. Launching Camoufox (a
    hardened Firefox) or a configured Chrome so that automated browsing is not
    trivially distinguishable from a human session. Used to study anti-bot
    systems and to keep research traffic from being misclassified.
  - CAPTCHA handling. Integration points for solving CAPTCHA challenges during
    authorised automation.
  - Runtime introspection. Dumping V8 heap contents, IndexedDB contents, worker
    scripts, and page data for offline analysis.

None of these are hidden, obfuscated or undocumented. The published `dist/` is
bundled output of the public TypeScript sources in the repository above; it is
not deliberately obfuscated to evade review. The repository is public and the
build is reproducible from source.


3. INTENDED LEGITIMATE USE
--------------------------

  - Malware and phishing analysis: deobfuscating malicious JavaScript, tracing
    what a suspicious page does, and extracting indicators from it.
  - Security research and vulnerability disclosure on systems the researcher is
    authorised to test.
  - Authorised penetration testing and red-team engagements, under a scope
    agreed with the target's owner.
  - Debugging and profiling the operator's own web applications, including
    production issues that only reproduce in a real browser.
  - Privacy and supply-chain review of third-party scripts embedded in sites the
    operator is responsible for.
  - Academic research, security education, and CTF competition.
  - Anti-fraud and bot-mitigation research: understanding how automation is
    detected, in order to build better defences.

Intended users are security researchers, reverse engineers, penetration
testers, and developers analysing code they are responsible for.


4. WHAT IT IS NOT FOR
---------------------

This package is intended for use only against systems and data the operator owns
or has explicit written authorisation to test. It must not be used for
unauthorised access, credential theft, fraud, harassment, or any other activity
prohibited by npm's Acceptable Use Policies.

The package contains no command-and-control capability, no credential
exfiltration, no self-propagation, no persistence mechanism, and no code that
targets a specific third party. It performs no network activity unless the
operator explicitly invokes a tool that does.


5. NOTES ON THINGS AUTOMATED SCANNING MAY FLAG
----------------------------------------------

  - `scripts/postinstall.cjs` is included in the published package. Outside a
    development checkout it exits immediately without doing anything: it only
    acts when it can see a `.git` directory, `src/index.ts` or `tsconfig.json`,
    none of which are published. In a dev checkout it may rebuild native
    modules (better-sqlite3, isolated-vm, koffi) whose ABI no longer matches the
    active Node version, and install git hooks via lefthook. It is not part of
    the installed runtime path.
  - Native dependencies (`better-sqlite3`, `isolated-vm`, `koffi`) are used for
    local storage, sandboxed evaluation, and FFI. `isolated-vm` in particular is
    a sandbox, not an escape from one.
  - Dynamic evaluation (`Runtime.evaluate`, sandboxed V8 contexts) is a core
    feature of the tool, not an obfuscation technique.
  - The package reads and writes files under the operator's working directory
    for captured artefacts and offloaded payloads.

If any of the above is a concern, we are glad to answer questions. The
maintainer can be reached through the repository's issue tracker at
https://github.com/vmoranv/jshookmcp/issues
